%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f7b71127f01acc641a3ca051454d27eb, type: 3}
  m_Name: HowToUse
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::VRSprayPainting.HelpContent
  pages:
  - title: Welcome
    body: 'This trainer teaches real spray painting, one skill at a time, and scores
      you like a real job.


      Before you start, clear a space around you (about
      2 x 2 m) and stand in the middle. You can do everything standing in one place.'
  - title: Controllers
    body: '<b>GRIP</b> (side button): pick up and hold the sprayer.

      <b>TRIGGER</b>
      (index finger): spray. Also presses menu buttons.

      <b>THUMBSTICK</b>
      up / down: narrower / wider spray (spray gun only).

      <b>B</b> or <b>Y</b>:
      clear all paint.


      To press a button on a panel: point at it with the
      controller and pull the trigger.'
  - title: Holding and spraying
    body: 'Pick up the sprayer from the stool on your right.


      <b>Spray can:</b>
      shake it hard up and down until the ball rattles freely.

      <b>Hold it
      15-25 cm</b> from the panel, pointing straight at it.

      <b>Move steadily</b>,
      about 30 cm per second, in side-to-side passes.

      Each pass <b>overlaps</b>
      the last by half.

      Start spraying just <b>before</b> the panel and stop
      just <b>after</b> it.'
  - title: Live help
    body: 'The <b>ring</b> on the panel shows your distance:

      <color=#4fd16a>GREEN</color>
      = right distance   <color=#ffa030>ORANGE</color> = too close   <color=#ffd84a>YELLOW</color>
      = too far   <color=#ff5a45>RED</color> = out of range.


      In lessons,
      a <b>number above the sprayer</b> shows your angle, distance, speed or how
      well the can is mixed.


      The <b>sign above the panel</b> shows when the
      paint is wet and when it is dry and ready for the next coat.'
  - title: Your report
    body: "After each job you get a scorecard:\n\u2022 <b>Score</b> against the pass
      mark.\n\u2022 <b>Film thickness</b> in \xB5m against the target: bare, thin,
      on target, heavy.\n\u2022 <b>Defects</b> and the points they cost: runs, dry
      spray, orange peel, missed spots.\n\u2022 <b>Technique</b> page: a bar per
      skill, a score per coat and a graph of your distance and angle.\n\n<b>Thickness
      map</b> colours the panel: blue = thin, green = good, red = too heavy.\nAll
      attempts are saved in <b>History</b>."
  - title: Safety
    body: 'Real spray paint is toxic and flammable.

      Always protect your lungs
      and eyes (a <b>full-face respirator</b> rated for paint does both, or a half-mask
      plus <b>safety goggles</b>), wear gloves, and spray in a <b>ventilated</b>
      area (booth fan on, or outdoors).


      The first lesson has you put the
      respirator on in VR: pick it up from the small table on your right and bring
      it to your face. Then point at the BOOTH FAN switch above the table and pull
      the trigger.'
